    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The organised campaign for women’s votes, usually dated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1848, won its first national victory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1893.</li><li>Suffragists used petitions, conventions, lobbying and marches; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the militant suffragettes smashed windows, set fires and went on hunger strike in prison.</li><li>Much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> gave women the vote around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, but many women of colour and colonial subjects waited decades longer.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>’s first election with women voters, in 1907, nineteen women won seats in the new parliament, the first women elected to a national legislature anywhere in the world.</p></aside>
    </section>

